  "body": {
    "id": "lem:finite-graph-primitives",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every finite canonically encoded input \\(I\\), all ancestor, descendant, nondescendant, district, strict-parent, d-separation, laid-back, serious-set, support, and compatibility predicates used by \\(\\operatorname{next}_I\\) are decidable.  Every enumerated candidate family is finite, and every nonempty such family has a unique canonically least member.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "InputI",
      "Gplus",
      "H",
      "V",
      "validH",
      "fixSeqH",
      "reachableH",
      "closureH",
      "laidbackH",
      "seriousH",
      "GcontextH",
      "encH"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:finite-categorical",
      "def:finite-graph-computations"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "By \\(\\textnormal{ass:finite-categorical}\\), the vertex set and each value set in \\(G^{+}\\) are finite.  A directed ancestor or descendant predicate is decided by enumerating directed paths of length at most the number of vertices; a district predicate is decided by enumerating bidirected paths of the same bounded length.  Strict parents are read from the finite edge table.  For d-separation, enumerate the finitely many simple paths and check the collider/noncollider condition after computing the finite ancestor set of the conditioning set.  The definitions of laid-backness, compatibility, and \\(\\operatorname{seriousSet}\\) are finite Boolean tests on selector coordinates.  The graph \\(G[s]\\) is obtained by a finite pass over the labelled edge list followed by finite deduplication.  Subsets, permutations, districts, contexts in \\(H\\), and supported triples therefore form finite encoded lists.  The bit-string encoding \\(\\operatorname{enc}_H\\) induces a total lexicographic order, so a nonempty finite list has exactly one least member.  Every assertion follows from these explicit finite procedures.",
